<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dionysius II of Syracuse</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 397–after 344 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> · Also known as Dionysius the Younger</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dionysius II of Syracuse, called the Younger, succeeded his father Dionysius I as tyrant of Syracuse in 367 BCE. Driven out by his kinsman Dion in 357, he took refuge in Locri Epizephyrii, his mother's city, and ruled it harshly until about 346, when he regained Syracuse. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timoleon">Timoleon</a> expelled him again in 344, and he ended his life in exile at Corinth.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Dionysius II was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysius-i-of-syracuse">Dionysius the Elder</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dori">Doris</a> of Locri. When his father died in 367 BCE he was under thirty and completely inexperienced in public affairs. He inherited supreme power and began to rule under the supervision of his uncle Dion. Dion disapproved of the young man's lavishly dissolute lifestyle and invited his own teacher, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a>, to visit Syracuse. Together they tried to make the government more moderate, with Dionysius as an ideal philosopher-king, as Plato's Seventh Letter describes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Under the influence of Dion's opponents Dionysius conspired with the historian Philistus and banished his uncle, taking complete power in 366 BCE. Without Dion his rule grew unpopular, since he was mostly incompetent at governing men and commanding soldiers. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a> appealed for Dion's return, Dionysius interfered with Dion's property and finances and gave his wife to another man. In the Seventh Letter Plato describes a test of Dionysius's philosophical progress, which, in Plato's account, he failed by claiming to know already the most important matters and by disdaining further training.</p><p>Dion formed a small army at Zacynthus and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> in 357 BCE, to the delight of the Syracusans. Dionysius was then at Caulonia in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and Dion took all of Syracuse except the island citadel. Dionysius sailed back and tried attacks and peace treaties, but without success, and then left for Locri, putting his son Apollocrates in charge of the citadel. In Locri he made himself tyrant and treated the people with great cruelty.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Dionysius did not return to Syracuse until 346 BCE, eight years after Dion's assassination by his officers. When he left Locri the remaining troops were driven out and the locals took revenge on his wife and daughters. He regained power in Syracuse only because of its political instability: many other cities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> had defected and were ruled by local tyrants, and several joined the Syracusans in an attack that forced him back into the citadel.</p><p>In 344 BCE Timoleon arrived and began his invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>. Dionysius, aware that he could not win and out of respect for Timoleon, arranged the surrender of the citadel and received safe passage to Corinth. He lived there in increasingly miserable conditions. The anecdote of the Sword of Damocles is often wrongly attributed to him, since it concerns his father, and a tradition makes him a schoolteacher at Corinth.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dionysius II of Syracuse” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His son <b>Dionysius II of Syracuse</b> was driven out by Timoleon in 344 BCE, and Agathocles of Syracuse, who seized power in 317 BCE, took the title of king in 304 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/government/tyranny">Tyranny · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>It was Syracuse's closest ally: Dionysius I married a Locrian woman, and <b>Dionysius II</b> ruled the city harshly from 356 to 346 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/locri-epizephyrii">Locri Epizephyrii</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Syracuse, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Ancient Corinth, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.07,15.29&amp;year=-363" aria-label="Syracuse on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4933 829 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Dionysius II of Syracuse"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5383 1099h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5383 1099h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.07,15.29&amp;year=-363">What was here in 363 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
